---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const formatted = new Date('2026-08-01').toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' });
---
<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Before doing more marketing, you need to understand the business better"
  description="Why before publishing more content you need to solve the business strategy first. An analysis from strategic marketing consulting."
>
  <article class="section" style="padding-top: 100px">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="label" style="color: var(--gold)">Strategy · {formatted}</p>
      <h1 class="display" style="font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 52px); margin: 16px 0 8px">Before doing more marketing, you need to understand the business better</h1>
      <p style="font-style: italic; color: rgba(44,44,44,0.6); margin-bottom: 32px">Why most marketing problems are, in reality, unresolved strategy problems</p>

      <p>When a brand feels that "marketing isn't working," the first question is almost never the right one. The usual question is: what should we publish? The question that actually moves something is different: what should this brand mean to the person who needs it?</p>

      <h2>The symptom isn't the problem</h2>
      <p>This is the case of Sayumarketing, an agency that had been operating for about a year without consistent client acquisition. The initial diagnosis could have stopped at "it needs more presence, more content, more visibility." That wasn't it. The problem was earlier — in the commercial foundations: positioning, value proposition, offer and pricing, none of which had been reviewed from the start.</p>

      <h2>What changes when you start with the business</h2>
      <p>The work wasn't about producing more marketing pieces, but about sorting out what was underneath: redefining positioning, structuring the value proposition, reviewing pricing, organizing the offer and adjusting commercial communication toward clearer acquisition. Only then did the marketing have something solid to communicate.</p>

      <h2>Practical application</h2>
      <p>The result was measured within a month: Sayumarketing landed its first two clients. Not by publishing more, but by better understanding what it was selling, to whom, and why that option should be chosen over any other. It's the same sequence I apply in any diagnosis: positioning → offer → pricing → communication → acquisition. Marketing is the last phase, not the first.</p>

      <a href="/en/contacto" class="btn" style="margin-top: 40px; display: inline-block">Let's talk about your business →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose :global(h2) { font-size: 26px; margin: 40px 0 12px; }
  .prose :global(p) { margin: 0 0 16px; }
</style>
